WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:07.520
Any communications, whether it's a website&nbsp;
or a video or even a circus, requires knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.960
your audience and adjusting the presentation to&nbsp;
them. For instance, you're doing a circus, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.760
for kids, of course you're going to go full-on&nbsp;
Ringling Brothers with lions and tigers and bears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.200
and clowns. But if you have a more sophisticated&nbsp;
audience, you might have a little more nuance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.680
and artistry. A little more Cirque Du Soleil than&nbsp;
Ringling Brothers. What do we need to know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.120
our audience for visualization? It's really&nbsp;
the same as for any communications challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.400
We need to understand their culture, their&nbsp;
level of expertise, and a few other things.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.160
First, culture. Your audience comes from a certain&nbsp;
place in the world. It affects their language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.240
it affects their perspective, it affects their&nbsp;
context, so many different things. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.440
think about, what does a wedding look like?&nbsp;
Sometimes it looks like this or like this or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.840
like this. It affects the colors that are used.&nbsp;
Color theory's its own big conversation that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.560
not going to cover in detail in this course,&nbsp;
but culture's an important influence on color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:18.160
amongst other things. For instance, what does a&nbsp;
wedding dress look like? Is it white? Is it red?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.600
And remember that, what looks odd in one culture&nbsp;
might be completely normal in another one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.320
Another thing culture influences is narrative&nbsp;
context. Does your audience know the underlying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.160
story of what you're talking about? For instance,&nbsp;
let's say you're creating a visualization of Wayne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.000
Gretzky statistics. Wayne Gretzky was probably&nbsp;
the greatest hockey player who ever lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.600
If you're doing this visualization for people&nbsp;
in northern countries who play a lot of hockey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.840
you probably don't need to set a lot of context.&nbsp;
You don't need to explain who Wayne Gretzky is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.680
But, if it's a visualization for people in,&nbsp;
maybe, Brazil or Madagascar, they might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.800
have as much of that context. You might need to&nbsp;
set up the story a little bit better for them.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.200
The next one is level of expertise. If you're&nbsp;
creating a project and it's for people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.520
experts in an industry, you're going to approach&nbsp;
it in one way. Less context, less hand-holding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.040
maybe even use different language, different&nbsp;
types of words, more lingo. If your audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.600
isn't quite as informed on the subject, you're&nbsp;
going to provide more background information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.800
more hand-holding. It's going to be like,&nbsp;
maybe, a shallower story with less detail.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.880
There's also what it is called the&nbsp;
consumption context or channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.720
Your work isn't just for fun or self-admiration,&nbsp;
you want to publish something somewhere somehow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.720
So, whether you're creating a visualization for&nbsp;
the New York Times or the Daily Show or a Bill's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.160
Blog for data dorks, it's going to change&nbsp;
your approach. One might be more serious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.040
Maybe a higher standard of excellence,&nbsp;
maybe more statistical integrity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.640
The other might be more irreverent. You&nbsp;
might have lower journalistic standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.320
You might round to fewer decimal&nbsp;
places with your numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.080
It's not about being less accurate, but you may&nbsp;
be less detail-oriented in certain contexts.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.640
Another issue is accessibility. Data&nbsp;
visualization is all about visualization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.960
it's all about the eye. We're only&nbsp;
dealing with sighted people, correct?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.000
Well, yes and no. You could say that you could&nbsp;
create a, quote, unquote, data visualization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.760
for the blinds, but really it's just audio. Maybe&nbsp;
there are other ways of thinking about this field.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.840
But there are other accessibility issues also,&nbsp;
even if you don't go quite that far, the big one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:50.480
being colorblindness. From one to 3% of people are&nbsp;
colorblind, and there's an especially common form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.120
called red-green colorblindness where people have&nbsp;
difficulty distinguishing between red and green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.720
Just think about stock market charts where red&nbsp;
is always used to mean prices going down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.320
green for prices going up. Think about what that&nbsp;
might mean for the one to 3% of people who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.680
trouble distinguishing between those two colors.&nbsp;
Luckily, in trying to channel your audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.600
and understand what they're going through,&nbsp;
there's a great website to help you with this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.920
There's this website where you can&nbsp;
actually take an image and upload it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.040
You can select an image from your hard drive, and&nbsp;
it's going to show you what the chart looks like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.520
This is the original image over here. And then&nbsp;
you can actually go in and click on these little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.160
radio buttons and see what it would look like for&nbsp;
people with different forms of colorblindness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.080
It can help you channel your audience and see&nbsp;
what they would see. It's a great way to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.240
look at your work and test it and make sure that&nbsp;
you're being friendly to all of your audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.720
There are other things to think about when&nbsp;
you're thinking about the visually impaired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.960
A big one being contrast and font size, creating&nbsp;
your charts with enough contrast to be easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:03.120
seen, making sure the font size is large enough&nbsp;
to be read. Forget about visual impairment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.560
just think about your audience. If they tend&nbsp;
to be over 40 years old, make sure your font&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.240
sizes aren't 10-point. If your chart is for&nbsp;
college kids, don't worry about it so much.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.600
The next thing to keep in mind is whether or&nbsp;
not your audience consists of true believers or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.680
skeptics, or more often than not, it's both, but&nbsp;
you have to be aware of it. If you're visualizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.200
data, let's say, about climate change, to&nbsp;
climate scientists, it'll be received differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.520
than if you're sharing the same data with a&nbsp;
publication targeting the coal mining industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.880
This is a dangerous lesson in that you don't want&nbsp;
to overthink how much convincing you need to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.880
otherwise it might bias what you share and how&nbsp;
you frame it. On the other hand, being aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.360
whether you're sharing information to change&nbsp;
minds or simply to provide the truth, is good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.480
to understand, because it might affect how deeply&nbsp;
you dive and how much interactivity you provide.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.800
And finally, there is the action that you want&nbsp;
your audience to take. You're sharing your content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.760
for an action, a reaction from your audience.&nbsp;
You need to know what you're looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.640
what action you're looking for to design better&nbsp;
outcomes. Do you want someone to call their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:19.040
congressman after looking at your visualization?&nbsp;
Do you want them to answer a poll question? Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:25.360
want them to share it on social? Do you want to&nbsp;
inspire them in some other way? If you constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.840
ask yourself if your design is leading toward&nbsp;
this outcome, you're more likely to achieve it.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.320
These are all the things you have to understand&nbsp;
about your audience when you're creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.760
visualizations for them, but it's more than that.&nbsp;
It's not just about understanding, it's about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:46.000
channeling them. It's about really getting inside&nbsp;
their head. You don't want to ignore their culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.480
and accessibility issues and all these other&nbsp;
things, you want to actually feel their pain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.800
You want to understand it as though you are them.&nbsp;
You'll be less likely to be influenced by bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.480
for instance because you're going to feel&nbsp;
their skepticism, their arguments against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.760
what you're showing them. It's going to help you&nbsp;
articulate more clearly and factually if you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:13.600
really imagine being them. Imagine that you are&nbsp;
them and you'll communicate more meaningfully.

